ukraine's top prosecutor is due to meet  her counterpart from the international  criminal court in the hague later today  they'll be looking at how best to  coordinate investigations into alleged  war crimes by russian soldiers in  ukraine some trials have already begun  thanks to meticulous documentation on  the ground but investigating potential  war crimes could be more of a challenge  in regions where russia has taken  control  a warning our next report contains some  disturbing images from the start  images that no one should have to see  civilians strewn across a street in  butcher  the immediate aftermath of a strike on a  maternity hospital in mariupol  a mass grave holding the unidentified  bodies of fathers mothers brothers  sisters grandparents and children  after ukrainian fighters pushed russian  forces back from towns and villages  they'd occupied  evidence of atrocities began to pile up  all being documented by human rights  groups ukrainian officials and  journalists on the ground  now almost two months later ukraine's  prosecutor general says investigations  are underway into well over 10 000  suspected war crimes  ranging from pillaging and looting rape  and summary execution to indiscriminate  bombing of civilian infrastructure  the first war crimes trial concluded  last week resulting in a guilty verdict  for a 21 year old russian soldier  charged with shooting an unarmed  ukrainian man  but in the eastern donbass region where  the war is still being fought tracking  down evidence and prosecuting potential  war crimes could prove a lot more  difficult  in the southern city of mariupol aerial  pictures showed the word children dogged  outside the theater building that was  hit by russian airstrikes in march  an associated press investigation  suggests as many as 600 people may have  been killed there as they sought shelter  ukraine says russia knew it was a  civilian target  now mariupol is in russian hands  and an independent investigation at the  site has been impossible  exactly who and how many people died  there may never be known  in april satellite images showed  apparent mass graves near the city  local officials say up to 9 000 bodies  could be buried there victims of russian  aggression they say  now russia is focusing its assault on  the luhansk region  the fear is if more towns and cities  fall under russian control the harder it  will become to investigate potential war  crimes and hold anyone there to account  earlier i spoke to hugh williamson from  human rights watch in berlin and i asked  him how long it could actually take to  investigate the thousands of suspected  war crimes in ukraine  good morning and thanks for having me um  i certainly hope so  obviously it's difficult to estimate how  long it's going to take it's important  that  investigations teams have already  started as you heard in your report the  ukrainian prosecutor's office is busy  with this issue  international criminal court has sent  experts to ukraine as well it's  important that these teams are well  coordinated  sharing resources supporting the  prosecutor's office in the work they're  doing  um and on that basis  um  the work needs to be thorough and  independent but obviously needs to move  forward as fast as possible  so what are the the problems that  investigators are confronting right now  while they're collecting evidence of  potential war crimes  i mean evidently where this is happening  in the midst of a war and that poses  huge problems security problems for  those investigating  problems of  securing the evidence  allowing access for forensic experts  and so on  ukrainian authorities are obviously  extremely stretched and therefore they  do need  support from other prosecution uh teams  and so on other other european countries  have also sent teams to to ukraine so  resources are an important point but  also independence is a vital point as  well um  you know  ukraine is in the middle of a war and  therefore  sense a sense of sort of  national pride and anti-russian  sentiment is obviously strong  nevertheless the prosecutor's office  needs to assess things through the  judicial lens and make sure that trials  are free and fair which is a challenging  task for them in a war situation but  it's absolutely vital for that to happen  if the the results and the verdicts are  to be seen as credible in the longer  term  now we see more and more evidence of uh  alleged war crimes in regions that  russian troops have left should we  expect any investigations to take place  in the area still held by the russians  um i mean it is obviously difficult for  difficult if not impossible for  investigators to go there regarding  russian war crimes nevertheless  um it's possible to gather evidence  human rights watch for instance in  mariopol one of the main cities now  seemingly under russian control has been  gathering evidence for weeks and weeks  there  both  remotely through  assessing videos photos that have been  placed online and so on looking at  patterns of bombing by russia  for instance but also by interviewing  people who have left maori or poll we've  done a whole range of interviews with  people in towns relatively nearby so  it's certainly possible to gather  evidence  in real time as it were and to make that  available to prosecutors so they can  start building cases even for  in areas which are still under russian  control  hugh williamson there from human rights  watch thank you very much  thanks for your time
 

